ZIF-8, a subclass of metal organic frameworks (MOFs), was employed as the CuO carriers because of its high surface areas and good dispersibility. A novel antibacterial agent $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ was synthesized by environmentally-friendly direct calcination strategy, and introduced into the composite double-layer films for packing materials. The double-layer films were prepared via solution casting method with polylactic acid (PLA) and polyvinyl alcohol (PVA)-quaternary ammonium chitosan as the matrix of outer layer and inner layer, respectively; and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ nanoparticles were introduced into the PVA-quaternary ammonium chitosan layer. The double-layer films exhibited superior antibacterial activity resulted from the uniform dispersion of CuO by ZIF-8 carriers. The elongation at break was enhanced and up to $17.13\%$ , about 2.4-fold that of PLA films. Meanwhile, the films provided low water vapor permeability and strong UV-barrier ability which were attributed to the lay-by-layer casting, $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ doping and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ addition. Cherry tomato preservation experiment revealed that the composite films retarded the growth of harmful microorganisms on the fruit surface. MTT assay confirmed the cytocompatibility of the films. The easily fabricated double-layer films presented potential pos­ sibility in the field of biodegradable food packaging.
## 1. Introduction
Bacterial and fungal contamination and the uncontrolled growth of these microorganisms are the main causes of food spoilage and indi­ rectly have adverse effects on humans, so the development of food packaging materials with antimicrobial performance is of increasing interest to researchers [1,2]. Petroleum-based plastic materials are commonly used for food packaging, but their slow degradation process has a negative impact on the environment, making the use of biode­ gradable polymer-based packaging materials a pressing need [3–5].
Polylactic acid (PLA) is a thermoplastic aliphatic polyester, and it dominates the biodegradable polymers market because of its superior processability, high transparency, rigidity, relatively low water vapor transmission rate and cost [6–8]. However, the low toughness limits its application in food packaging [3]. To overcome its shortcomings, a modified PLA is expected by means of modification technique, such as filling with nanoparticles and layer-by-layer casting, etc. Arrieta et al. [9] reported a composite film by melt extrusion using PLA as the matrix and cellulose nanocrystals as the bio-based reinforcement; this com­ posite film exhibited stronger elongation at break compared with that of PLA film, but the transparency and water vapor barrier properties were substantially reduced. Due to the excellent elongation at break $(>200\%)$ ) of polyvinyl alcohol (PVA), Dong et al. [10] prepared multilayer films by assembling PLA and PVA, and the mechanical properties of the com­ posite films were enhanced evidently; at the same time, the combination of PLA and PVA provided complementary barrier properties [11]. Hydroxypropyl trimethyl ammonium chloride chitosan (HACC), a qua­ ternary ammonium chitosan derivative, could enhance the adhesion between PVA coating and plastic substrates as reported before [12], but the high content addition of water soluble HACC in the matrix tended to increase the water adsorption and result in the films swelling, leading to deteriorated films stability in humid environments.
For food packing materials antibacterial performance is a prerequi­ site to prevent food from bacterial and fungal contamination. Inorganic nanoparticles, especially metal and semiconductor metal oxide nano­ particles, have attracted great interest due to their superiority in anti­ bacterial action even at extremely low concentrations, and simplicity of production [13]. Copper oxide (CuO) nanoparticles show broadspectrum antibacterial activity against various Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria and exert biocidal effects mainly through con­ tact antibacterial, release of metal ions, and formation of reactive oxy­ gen species such as hydroxides, superoxide ions and hydrogen peroxide [14,15]. However, the nano-sized $\mathtt{C u O}$ particles suffers from the prob­ lem of easy agglomeration [16] and so their disperse in matrix is one of the key problems to be solved.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are novel multifunctional pre­ cursor composed of metal ions (such as $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Mo}^{2+}$ ) or metal clusters and organic bridging ligands. They possess porous structure, ultra-high surface area and structural diversity, and more importantly, MOFs can control the limited growth of metal nano­ particles, prevent their aggregation. As the metal doping is facile and uniform, it can be used as an effective carrier for metal nanoparticles [17]. ZIF-8, a subclass of MOFs consisting of zinc metal ions and 2-meth­ ylimidazole ligands, is chemically stable and well dispersed in aqueous and alkaline media with a reported surface area of up to about 1800 $\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ [18,19]. In this sense the loading of CuO on ZIF-8 carriers $(\mathsf{C u O@Z I F{-}8})$ could be a sound solution to CuO particles aggregation.
The conversion of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ to $\mathtt{C u O}$ was usually realized by the addition of NaOH as reported before [14,20–23].
$$
\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+2\mathrm{OH}^{-}=\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\downarrow
$$
$$
\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}{\stackrel{\Delta}{=}}\mathrm{CuO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
This method generated a large amount of free CuO impurities, which was not favorable for the synthesis of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ . It was reported that copper nitrate converts to CuO at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [24].
$$
2\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\triangleq}2\mathrm{CuO}+4\mathrm{NO}_{2}\uparrow+2\mathrm{O}_{2}\uparrow
$$
While ZIF-8 does not pyrolyze at this temperature [25]. In this re­ gard, we proposed an environmentally friendly, and simple method to prepare high-purity antimicrobial nanocomposites $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF-8~}\mathrm{~NPs}.$ that is, the direct calcination of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ loaded ZIF-8 $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}@\mathsf{Z I F}.$ - 8) at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ would produce CuO@ZIF-8 NPs without free CuO aggregates.
In this study, we designed composite double-layer films via solution casting method with PLA and PVA-HACC as the matrix of outer layer and inner layer, respectively. $1\%$ (w/w) $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were added into the PLA layer, and antibacterial agent $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ were introduced into the PVA-HACC inner layer, forming PLA- $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ –1/PVA-HACC-CuO@ZIF-8 (PT-1/PHZ) double-layer films (Scheme 1). The mechanical properties, water vapor permeability, UV-barrier properties，antibacterial activity and cytotoxicity evaluation of the composite films were investigated in detail. And a preliminary evaluation of the practical application in the food preservation of cherry tomatoes was also carried out.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{(Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $99.99\%$ metals basis), 2- methylimidazole (2-MI, $98\%$ , copper nitrate trihydrate (Cu $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ $99.99\%$ metals basis), titanium dioxide $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $40\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ) and polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, polymerization degree, 1799; hydrolysis de­ gree, $99\%$ ) were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (China). Polylactic acid (PLA, average molecular weight, $\sim80000)$ and hydroxypropyl trimethyl ammonium chloride chitosan (HACC, substitution degree $\geq92.5\%$ ) were obtained from Shanghai
Macklin Biochemical Technology Co. Ltd. Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus ATCC 6538) and Escherichia coli (E. coli ATCC 25922) were pro­ vided by Shanghai Luwei Microbial Sci. & Tech. Co. Ltd. The cherry tomatoes were purchased from the market in Taiyuan. The rest chemical reagents, if not explicitly mentioned, were supplied by Alfa Aesar.
## 2.2. Preparation of CuO@ZIF-8 nanoparticles
ZIF-8 NPs were synthesized according to the reported method [26] with some improvements. Briefly, a methanolic solution of Zn $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1 mmol) was added dropwise to a methanolic solution of 2-MI (16 mmol) under stirring. The milky dispersion was obtained by continuous stirring for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ at room temperature; then the nanoparticles were collected from the milky dispersion by centrifugation (10,000 rpm), followed by washing three times with fresh methanol and drying overnight at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum oven.
ZIF-8 (0.1 g) was ultrasonically dispersed in anhydrous ethanol (40 mL), then $\phantom{-}0.04g$ of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added in. Next, the mixture was ultrasonically dispersed for $10\mathrm{min}$ followed by stirring for $6\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature to obtain a light blue dispersion. Subsequently, the dispersion was centrifuged at $10000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . After washing with anhydrous ethanol $(15~\mathrm{mL})$ and drying in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight, the isolated solid was ground and calcined at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of the film-forming solutions
$_{0.8\mathrm{~g~}}$ of PLA and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs $(1\%$ , w/w) were added to $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of chloroform and stirred for about $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature until a ho­ mogeneous dispersion was obtained. With 1 wt percent of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NPs}$ , the mixture of PLA and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was named as PT-1.
$5\%$ $(w/\nu)$ PVA particles were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , after which the PVA solution was cooled down to room temperature. The calculated amount of CuO@ZIF-8 NPs was dispersed in $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ of $40\ \mathrm{vol}\%$ aqueous alcohol solution to form $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ dispersion, then this dispersion was mixed with PVA solution followed by stirring for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature to form mixture A. Next, $0.20\mathrm{g}$ of HACC and $_{0.12\mathrm{~g~}}$ of glycerol were dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water to form solution B. Finally, mixture A was added into solution B, thus the PVA-HACC-CuO@ZIF-8 NPs (named as PHZ) film-forming so­ lution was obtained after stirring at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.4. Preparation of the composite double-layer films PT-1/PHZ
Firstly, $15\mathrm{mL}$ of PT-1 film-forming solution was casted onto the glass plate using a film applicator, and left to evaporate the solvent at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ , thus solid PT-1 films was produced and served as the outer layer. Subsequently, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of PHZ film-forming solution was poured onto PT-1 films surface and allowed to dry with the same pro­ cess. The PHZ film containing 0, 1, 3, 5, and 7 wt percent of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs were conveniently named as PHZ-0, PHZ-1, PHZ-3, PHZ-5, and PHZ-7, respectively, and served as the inner layer. Finally, the asprepared composite films (named as PT-1/PHZ) were placed in an oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to evaporate chloroform, followed by sealing for conservation at room temperature for at least $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.5. Characterization
## 2.5.1. X-ray diffractometry (XRD) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM)
The X-ray diffraction patterns were characterized by X-ray diffrac tometer (Rigaku D/max 2500 V) with Nickel-filtered Cu Kα radiation (λ $=0.154~\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{\Omega}$ , an angular range of $2\Theta=5–80^{\circ}$ , and a scanning speed of $4^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and energy-dispersive Xray spectroscope (EDS) were performed with an S-4800 instrument (Hitachi, Japan) at an accelerating voltage of $20\mathrm{kV}$ [27]. The surfaces of the samples were sprayed with gold before examination.
## 2.5.2. Transmission electron microscope (TEM)
TEM images were obtained via a Tecnai G2 F20S-TWIN TEM mi­ croscope (FEI Ltd., USA) operated at $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV}}$ .
## 2.5.3. Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy
The FT-IR spectral images were measured by attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) spectrophotometer. The corresponding spectra were recorded at wave numbers 400–4000 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ with a resolution of $4\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [28].
## 2.5.4. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and inductively coupled plasma spectrometer (ICP)
XPS analysis was performed using an X-ray photoelectron spec­ trometer (ThermoFischer, ESCALAB 250Xi, USA). The excitation source was Al Kα rays $(h\nu=1486.6\mathrm{eV})$ , the operating voltage was $12.5\mathrm{kV}$ , the test flux energy (Passing-Energy) was $50\mathrm{eV}$ for the full spectrum and 20 eV for the narrow spectrum, with a step size of $0.05\mathrm{eV}$ , a dwell time of $40{-}50~\mathrm{ms}$ , and a charge correction using $\mathrm{C1}s=284.80\ \mathrm{eV}$ binding en­ ergy as the energy standard.
The released ions concentration was detected via inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, OPTIMA $2\times$ 00/5000, Perkin Elmer Inc., U. S.).
## 2.5.5. Mechanical properties
The mechanical properties of the films were measured according to ISO 527-3 [29] using a universal testing machine (YC-125, Shanghai Yuhang Machinery, China). The films samples were cut into $5\mathrm{cm}\times2\mathrm{cm}$ long strips and the machine was made to run in tensile mode with a crosshead speed of $30\mathrm{mm/min}$ . At least 10 samples were tested in each group.
2.5.6. Water vapor permeability (WVP) and water contact angle (WCA) The water vapor permeability of the films was determined by the weight method according to the American Society for Testing Materials (ASTM) E96–95 [30]. Dried calcium chloride $(10\mathrm{g})$ was added to a glass with a diameter of $27\mathrm{mm}$ and a depth of $56~\mathrm{mm}$ to maintain a relative humidity of $0\%$ in the top space of the glass, and then the films sample $(4\mathrm{cm}\times4\mathrm{cm})$ was sealed at the mouth of the glass with molten paraffin. The glass was placed in a desiccator containing saturated sodium chlo­ ride to maintain a relative humidity of $75\%$ $(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . At least 3 samples were tested in each group. The WVP was calculated in $\mathbf{g\cdotm}/\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}s{\cdot}\mathbf{Pa}$ as follows:
$$
\mathrm{WVP}=\frac{\Delta\boldsymbol{m}\times\boldsymbol{e}}{t\times\boldsymbol{S}\times\boldsymbol{P}}
$$
where $\varDelta m\left(g\right)$ is the mass change of the glass within time t (s), $e$ (m) is the film thickness, $P$ (Pa) is the saturation pressure, and $\scriptstyle{S(\mathrm{cm}^{2})}$ is area of the glass mouth.
The hydrophobicity of the films was analyzed using a contact angle meter (Shanghai Zhongchen Digtal Technology Instruments Co., Ltd., China). The test was repeated 5 times for each sample.
## 2.5.7. UV–vis spectroscopy analysis
The visible and UV light transmittance of the films in the wavelength range of $200{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ was measured using a UV–Visible spectropho­ tometer (UV-1800, Shanghai Precision Instruments Co., Ltd., China).
## 2.6. Antibacterial activities
## 2.6.1. Inhibition circle test
The inhibition circle method was used to assess the antibacterial activity of ZIF-8, CuO and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ against Gram-negative $E$ . coli and Gram-positive S. aureus bacteria, respectively [31]. Prior to testing, bacteria were inoculated in nutrient broth at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ in an aerobic environment. Then the cultured bacteria suspensions were diluted with phosphate buffer saline (PBS) solution to reach the bacteria concentra­ tion of ${\sim}10^{6}$ colony forming units per milliliter $\mathrm{(CFU/mL)}$ . Next, $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of bacteria suspension $(\sim10^{6}\mathrm{CFU/mL})$ was spread onto the surface of nutrient agar. Subsequently, round filter papers were soaked in 1000 ppm of antimicrobial agent dispersions for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and then placed on the surface of the nutrient agar plate. After being cultured in $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ , the inhibition circle was observed and recorded.
## 2.6.2. Antibacterial properties of composite films
A colony counting method was used to evaluate the antibacterial activity of the films [32]. $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of bacteria $(-10^{5}\mathrm{CFU/mL})$ was spread onto the surface of the films $(3\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 3\ \mathrm{cm})$ , and the acquired samples were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then the bacteria were washed down from the films surface with $300~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of PBS solution, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of which was spread on a nutrient agar plate and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for another $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , followed by the surviving bacterial colonies counting. The bacterial in­ hibition rate $(\%)$ could be calculated as:
$$
{\mathrm{Inhibition~rate}}=(X-Y)/X\times100\%
$$
where $X$ was the number of colonies on PT-1 films, and $Y$ was the number of colonies on PT-1/PHZ films.
## 2.6.3. Cytotoxicity test
The cytotoxicity of composite films was measured by the MTT (3- (4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide) assay [33]. Mouse embryonic fibroblasts (NIH-3T3) cells (5000 cells/well) were plated in 96-well plate and cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a maintained humid atmosphere with $5\%$ carbon dioxide. The sterile film samples $\mathrm{(2mg/mL)}$ were put into 96-well plates and incubated for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The films were removed, and the medium was replaced with fresh medium. Then, $20\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of MTT solution was added into each well and incubated for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . Sub­ sequently, the medium was removed and replaced with DMSO and mixed to dissolve the formazan crystals. Cells cultured in the medium were used as a negative control, and the cell survival rate was defined as $100\%$ . Each well's absorbance was measured at $492\mathrm{nm}$ using an enzyme marker analyzer (SM-3, China). The cell survival rate was calculated according to the formula:
$$
{\mathrm{Cell~viability}}={\frac{\mathrm{OD}_{\mathrm{sample}}}{\mathrm{OD}_{\mathrm{control}}}}\times100\%
$$
where $\mathrm{\Gamma_{OD_{sample}}}$ represents the absorbance of the test sample, ODcontrol represents the absorbance of the blank control group.
$40~\mathrm{mg}$ of PT-1/PHZ-5 composite double-layer film was immersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water and the supernatant was extracted after $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ of gentle vibration. The ions concentrations in the supernatant were detected via inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer [34].
## 2.7. Cherry tomatoes preservation experiment
Cherry tomatoes were selected to evaluate the preservation capacity of composite films. The cherry tomatoes labeled with 1 served as the control, and those labeled with 2, 3, and 4 were samples covered with PT-1, PT-1/PHZ-0, and PT-1/PHZ-5 films, respectively. Photographs were taken and weights were recorded at 7 day intervals (0, 7, 14 and 21 days), the weight loss rate is calculated as [35]:
where $\alpha$ is the weight of the fresh cherry tomatoes and $\beta$ is the weight of the preserved ones.
After storage for 7, 14 and 21 days, the cherry tomatoes were immersed in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of sterile peptone $(0.1\%w/\nu)$ . Firstly, the mixture was stirred to separate the microorganisms from the surface of the cherry tomatoes. Next, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the bacterial solution was spread onto the surface of nutrient agar and incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . At last, colonies on the plates were counted and the total viable count (TVC) was expressed as $\mathrm{CFU/g}$ [36].
## 2.8. Statistical analysis
All the data were expressed as means $\pm$ standard deviation (SD). All experiments were performed at least 3 times. Two-sample equal vari­ ance t-test was used to analyze the significant differences.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of CuO@ZIF-8 nanoparticles
Fig. 1 showed the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and trans­ mission electron microscopy (TEM) images of ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs. ZIF-8 exhibited typical hexahedral morphology with particle size of about $80\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1a, b and c). CuO, formed by calcination of copper nitrate at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , attached to the surface of ZIF-8, and resulted in a rough surface morphology lacking edge-sharpness (Fig. 1d, e and f). Notably, almost no free CuO particles aggregates were observed, which may attribute to the dispersion by ZIF-8 carriers. At last, the particle size of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ was almost the same as ZIF-8, and no obvious agglomera­ tion was observed for the two samples.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was employed to certify the structure and composition of CuO, ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ (Fig. 2a). As mentioned before, we proposed a novel strategy to prepare high-purity $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs, that is, copper nitrate concerted to CuO by calcination at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ . To investigate the crystal structure stability of ZIF-8 before and after calcination, ZIF-8 NPs was allowed to undergo the same calcination process, that is, they were calcinated at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ (named as C-ZIF8), and their XRD patters was also recorded in Fig. 2a. The XRD pattern of ZIF-8 matched well with that in the published report [18]. For C-ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ , typical diffraction peaks of ZIF-8 were observed, where the main peaks of the virgin ZIF-8 at $2\uptheta=7.30^{\circ}$ , $10.35^{\circ}$ , $12.70^{\circ}$ , $14.80^{\circ}$ , $16.40^{\circ}$ and $18.00^{\circ}$ corresponded to the planes (110), (200), (211), (220), (310) and (222), respectively, indicating the structure stability of ZIF-8 after calcination. However, no characteristic peaks of CuO were observed in $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ sample, which meant that $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ did not contain bulky free CuO particles but the well-dispersed CuO on ZIF-8. Nevertheless, it was concluded that no significant changes were detected for the ZIF-8 phase after CuO deposition [37].
The component compositions of CuO@ZIF-8 NPs were further detected by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra. The C, N, O, Zn, and Cu signals were shown from the XPS survey scan of $\operatorname{CuO}@\operatorname{ZIF}.$ - 8 (Fig. 2b), indicating the introduction of element Cu. And the ICP-OES result of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ showed that the Cu element content was $9.23\%$ and the Zn was $20.11\%$ . In the $\mathtt{C u2p}$ spectrum (Fig. 2c), satellite peak was a sign of the presence of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ; Cu 2p3/2 curve was fitted to two charac­ teristic peaks located at 933.7 and $932.6{\ }\mathrm{eV}$ , the peak at the binding energy of 933.7 eV was attributed to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , the peak at $932.6{\ }\mathrm{eV}$ was ascribed to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{1+}$ or ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0+}$ . The O 1s fine spectrum was fitted to two peaks located at $530.7\mathrm{eV}$ and $531.8\mathrm{eV}$ , which belonged to the ${0}^{2-}$ ion and the adsorbed $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ on the material surface, respectively. Therefore, combined with the Cu 2p and O 1s spectra, it is clear that the element Cu is mainly present in the form of $\mathtt{C u O}$ . In the $\mathsf{2p}$ spectra of Zn (Fig. 2d), the binding energies at 1044.7 and 1021.6 eV were ascribed to the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Zn}2\mathrm{p}1/2$ and $\mathtt{Z n2p3/2}$ , respectively, which indicated that Zn existed mainly in the form of $Z n^{2+}$ . In the N 1s spectra (Fig. 2e) of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8_{\cdot}$ , this curve was fitted to two characteristic peaks at $398.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $399.9\mathrm{eV}$ , which corresponded to $\mathsf{C-N}$ and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ in 2-methylimida­ zole, respectively. Thus, XRD and XPS results suggested the successful preparation of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ .
The antibacterial activity of ZIF-8, CuO and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}~\mathrm{NPs}$ was tested against S. aureus and E. coli, and the results were presented in Fig. 2g, h and i. It could be seen that the inhibitory radius of ZIF-8 against S. aureus and $E$ . coli was $0\ \mathrm{mm}$ ; the inhibitory radius of $\mathtt{C u O}$ against S. aureus and $E$ . coli was $1.31\pm0.14\mathrm{{mm}}$ and $1.44\pm0.12\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively; $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ showed inhibitory radius of $1.73\pm0.13\mathrm{mm}$ for S. aureus, and $2.07\pm0.15\mathrm{mm}$ for $E$ . coli. Based on these results, it could be concluded that ZIF-8 provided weak antibacterial activity, which was consistent with the results of previous studies [38]; while $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ exhibited increased antibacterial activity than that of $\mathtt{C u O}$ . CuO NPs attached on ZIF-8 was provided with increased dispersibility, avoiding heavy agglomeration, and so exhibited evidently enhanced antibacterial performance. The positively charged copper ions contacted the negatively charged bacteria through the attraction of electric charges, and then the copper ions entered the bacterial cells, resulted in the bacteria death.
## 3.2. Characterization of composite double-layer films PT-1/PHZ
## 3.2.1. FT-IR and XRD
As shown in Fig. 3a, the FT-IR spectra were employed to evaluate and characterize the interactions between the components in the composite films. For PHZ-0 single layer films, the absorption peaks at $3331~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $2928~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1099~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to stretching vibrations of polyvinyl alcohol hydroxyl groups, stretching vibrations of main chain aliphatic $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ and C–O bond stretching, respectively. The two peaks at 1572 and $1418~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ referred to the amide I and amide II bands of HACC, while the characteristic peaks at 1654 and $1471~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were ascribed to the $\tt N-H$ bonding and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bending of the trimethylamine group in HACC. For all the PT-1/PHZ composite double-layer films (PT1/PHZ-0, PT-1/PHZ-1, PT-1/PHZ-3, PT-1/PHZ-5 and PT-1/PHZ-7), the peak at $3331~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was shifted to $3317~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which was attributed to the interaction of hydrogen bonds between the PVA-HACC layer and the PLA layer (Scheme 1). Whereas, the introduction of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8\mathrm{NPs}$ into the PVA-HACC matrix resulted in negligible changes in the positions and intensities of the characteristic peaks, which indicated that only weak physical interactions were existed between the matrix and the nanoparticles.
The XRD patterns of films were shown in Fig. 3b. PHZ-0 films showed a sharp peak at $19.7^{\circ}$ , exhibiting the semi-crystalline nature of PVA. For PT-1/PHZ, the diffraction peaks at 7.4, 10.9, 12.9, 14.8, and $16.6^{\circ}$ belonged to the (110), (200), (211), (220), and (310) crystal planes of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ , and their intensities increased with the increase of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ content. Nevertheless, the characteristic peak of PVA remained constant for all PT-1/PHZ composite films, which indicated that the crystal structure of the matrix was unaffected by the addition of CuO@ZIF-8 NPs.
## 3.2.2. SEM
The surface morphology and cross-section of the films were studied by scanning electron microscopy. PT-1 (Fig. 4a) and PHZ-0 (Fig. 4b) surface morphology was relatively smooth, which meant that small amounts of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were well dispersed in the PLA matrix, and good compatibility was presented between PVA and HACC. With the intro­ duction of antibacterial agent $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ , the surface of PHZ inner layer exhibited rougher morphology, and the more the $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ content increased, the more obvious the rough trend became (Fig. 4c–f). Fig. $^{4g}$ and h showed the cross-section of the PT-1 and PT-1/ PHZ-5 films, respectively, and the outer layer (PT-1) thickness of 17.4 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and inner layer (PHZ-5) thickness of $14.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were observed for the composited double layer films. PHZ-5 layer, with $10\%$ (w/w) glycerol as plasticizer, bound firmly to the PT-1 layer and displayed a continuous and dense cross-section. On one hand, HACC in PHZ layer increased the viscosity of the casting solution, which was beneficial to enhance the adhesion between layers. On the other hand, both HACC and PVA were rich in -OH groups, which enabled their firm adhesion on the surface of PT-1 by the strong hydrogen-bonding interactions [8]. To reveal the dispersion of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ , the EDS mapping was carried out as shown in Fig. 4i–k, which indicated that Ti, Cu and $Z\mathrm{n}$ were uniformly dispersed in matrix. In view of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs, their incorpo­ ration into PLA matrix did not produce chemical interaction as confirmed by researchers [39,40].
## 3.2.3. Mechanical properties
The elongation at break, tensile strength and thickness of the com­ posite films were listed in Table 1. PLA and PT-1 films revealed low toughness, and the addition of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ showed negligible effect on it (the elongation at break was about $6.97\%$ and $6.83\%$ , respectively), and so modification was needed to overcome this shortcoming. After casting a layer of PHZ-0 film on the surface of the PT-1 film, the elongation at break of PT-1/PHZ-0 increased and the tensile strength decreased significantly, which was attributed to the fact that PHZ-0 layer with low tensile strength $(\sim13.46~\mathrm{MPa})$ had good physical adhesion to the PT-1 layer, so that the PHZ-0 layer provided a plasticizing effect on the PT1 layer [41]. The addition of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ into the PHZ-0 layer induced an increase in the internal consumption of the PVA-HACC matrix, which led to an increase in the tensile strength of the PHZ layer, and thus a continuous improvement in the tensile strength of the composite doublelayer films. The addition of an appropriate amount of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ improved the elongation at break of the composite double-layer films, which may ascribe to the fact that the addition of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ to the PVA-HACC matrix increased the interfacial interaction with the PT-1 layer, which in turn led to enhanced plasticizing effect [29]. The elon­ gation at break of the PT-1/PHZ-5 reached $17.13\%$ , which was about 2.4-fold that of PT-1, while the tensile strength decreased by nearly $17\%$ relative to PT-1. It could be concluded that the layer-by-layer casting and filling with nanoparticles in PLA films moderately improved their mechanical properties.
## 3.2.4. WVP and WCA
For food packaging materials, a strong barrier to water vapor was necessary, because the loss of moisture may reduce the freshness and accelerate the spoilage of packaged food, and so a low WVP was worthwhile. The WVP and WCA of films were summarized in Fig. 5a. A slight decrease in the water vapor transmission rate of the PT-1 films was observed by the addition of $1\%\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NPs}$ into the PLA matrix. The PHZ0 films displayed high water vapor permeability up to around $52.17\times\$ 10 $^{-11}{\bf g\cdot m}/{\bf m}^{2}{\bf\cdot s\cdot P a}$ , this value decreased remarkably after casting PT-1 layer $({\sim}4.80\ \times\ 10^{-11}\mathrm{\g{\cdot}m/m^{2}{\cdot}s{\cdot}P a)}$ . The WVP of the composite double-layer films decreased further with the increase of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs content, which was attributed to the fact that with the increase of hydrophobic $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs content, water molecules must migrate around or through the interface of impermeable $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs, leading to long and circuitous paths; at the same time, these nanoparticles could effectively fill the polymeric macromolecular network voids, making the penetration of water vapor more difficult. It could be seen in Fig. 5a, the WVP of PT-1/PHZ-5 $({\bf\ddot{\sim}}11.21\times10^{-11}\bf{g}.m/$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{m}}^{2}{\cdot}s{\cdot}\ensuremath{\mathbf{P}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{a}})$ decreased by $365.4\%$ and $40.3\%$ compared to PHZ-0 $(\sim52.17\$ $\times10^{-11}\mathrm{g}\mathrm{.m/m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}{\cdot}\mathrm{Pa})$ and PT-1/PHZ-0 $(-15.73\times10^{-11}{\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{m}}/{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{s}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Pa}})$ , respectively. PHZ layer casting on PT-1 films was effective in decreasing the WVP of PHZ films.
The water contact angle (WCA) test was conducted on PLA, PT-1 and PHZ-0 films and the inner layer (PHZ layer) of the composite doublelayer films. It was shown in Fig. 5a that the WCA of PLA and PT-1 films was close to $90^{\circ}$ , the value for PT-1/PHZ-0 films was around $70.51^{\circ}$ . With the increase of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ content, the WCA of composite double-layer films declined first and increased then. On the one hand, the addition of a small amount of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8~\mathrm{NPs}$ increased the surface roughness of the PHZ layer, leading to the decreased WCA (PT-1/PHZ-1 and PT-1/PHZ-3 films); on the other hand, continued addition of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs enhanced the WCA because of the hydrophobicity of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ . In conclusion, although the addition of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ into the PVA-HACC matrix increased the roughness of the PHZ films surface, at the same time it enhanced the hydrophobicity of the PHZ films, which was beneficial for reducing the WVP of PHZ and PT-1/PHZ films.
## 3.2.5. Light transmittance
The UV–vis spectra of the PLA and the composite films were shown in Fig. 5b. The transmission rates of PLA films in both the UV and visible regions were roughly around $98\%$ , indicating that PLA films were nearly transparent and had no blocking ability to UV light; the transmission rate of PHZ-0 films in the visible region was about $94\%$ , in the UV region it dropped to about $85\%$ , which was attributed to the weak UV blocking effect of PVA [16]. After the incorporation of nanoparticles, the trans­ mittance of visible light $(800-400~\mathrm{nm})$ ) was reduced due to the light blocking property of light-impenetrable NPs [42]. For the composite films with only $1\%\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs added (PT-1 and PT-1/PHZ-0), the trans­ mittance in the UV region $(400-200~\mathrm{{nm})}$ first decreased and then increased. This phenomenon may result from the fact that the inevitable aggregation of $1\%$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs blocked the UV mainly by reflect and scatter, which barriered the UV light in the region of $400{-}320~\mathrm{\nm}$ preferably. The transmittance in the UV region continued to decrease with the addition of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ , which suggested that $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ also presented certain barrier properties to UV light. This result indicated that the composite double-layer films possessed the ability to block UV light transmission and thus exhibited great potential for UV-blocking packaging materials.
## 3.2.6. Antibacterial properties
Gram-positive S. aureus and Gram-negative E. coli were selected as model bacteria to evaluate the sterilization performance of PT-1/PHZ films. As shown in Fig. 6, the antibacterial activity of PT-1 films was not significantly enhanced compared to PLA films, which resulted from the weak antibacterial properties of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs (its content was only $1\%$ , w/w) [39]; in fact, the role of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs in this study was mainly a UVblocking filler rather than an antibacterial agent. PT-1/PHZ-0 films displayed the antibacterial activity to some degree compared with PT-1 films, which attributed to the intrinsic antibacterial performance of HACC. Composite double-layer films exhibited enhanced antimicrobial activity with the increase of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{CuO}\ @\mathbf{ZIF}-8$ content (Fig. 6, Table 2), when the $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ content was $1\%$ , $3\%$ , $5\%$ and $7\%$ , the antibacterial ac­ tivity of the double-layer films against $E$ . coli was $56.8\pm2.4\%$ , $91.1\pm$ $0.7\%$ , $98.6\pm0.1\%$ and $99.6\pm0.1\%$ , respectively, and the value against S. aureus reached $90.8\pm0.9\%$ , $95.8\pm0.4\%$ , $99.2\pm0.2\%$ and $99.7\pm$ $0.1\%$ , respectively. On the one hand, copper oxide nanoparticles pro­ vided biocidal effects through contact antibacterial, release of metal ions and formation of reactive oxygen species (ROS); On the other hand, the uniform dispersion of CuO NPs by ZIF-8 carrier played the key role [43], making the composite films possible to serve as the antibacterial food packing material.
## 3.2.7. Cytotoxicity
The absence of cytotoxicity is an essential requirement of food packaging. The cytotoxicity of the composite films was evaluated using NIH 3T3 cells as an in vitro model (Fig. 7). Compared to the control group, the cell viability of NIH 3T3 cells treated with the composite films was above $90\%$ in all cases. Furthermore, no significant change of cell viability was observed with the increase of the CuO@ZIF-8 NPs con­ centration in composite films. At last, PT-1/PHZ-5 composite doublelayer film showed the release of Cu, Zn and Ti element of 1.90, 3.75 and $0.04~\mathrm{mg/L}$ , respectively according to the ICP-OES results, which were far below the cytotoxicity level threshold allowed for mammalian cells [44].
## 3.2.8. Cherry tomato preservation experiment
Taking into account of mechanical, barrier and antibacterial prop­ erties, PT-1/PHZ-5 were chosen as the antimicrobial packing films to undergo the food preservation ability evaluation. The photographs exhibited the appearance of cherry tomatoes with or without films covering at 7 days intervals, and those wrapped with PT-1/PHZ-5 films showed the fewest folds (Fig. 8a). At the same time, the PT-1/PHZ-5 films covered cherry tomatoes displayed the lowest weight loss rate (Fig. 8b), such a result was attributed to the highest water vapor barrier performance of PT-1/PHZ-5 films, which in turn led to low water loss of fruits. Fig. 8c demonstrated the microbial growth on the surface of cherry tomatoes uncovered and covered with PT-1, PT-1/PHZ-0 and PT1/PHZ-5 films at 7 days intervals. During the first week, the growth of microorganisms increased slowly, and it was accelerated over the next two weeks especially for uncovered and covered with PT-1 films. It was observed that those wrapped with PT-1/PHZ-5 composite films exhibi­ ted obvious microbial growth inhibition effect because of the superior antimicrobial activity of the double-layer films provided by $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs and HACC. Consequently, it could be concluded that PT-1/PHZ-5 film had the potential to extend the shelf life and retard the growth of harmful microorganisms on the fruit surface.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we synthesized the novel antimicrobial agent CuO@ZIF-8 NPs by a simple direct calcination strategy. The incorpo­ ration of the $\mathsf{C u O}@\mathsf{Z I F}{\cdot}8$ NPs into PLA/PVA-HACC double-layer films endowed the films with superior bactericidal efficiency because of the good dispersion of $\mathtt{C u O N P s}$ by ZIF-8 carriers. The elongation at break of PLA films was notably improved because of the plasticizing effect induced by PHZ layer casting. The strong barrier to water vapor was realized by casting of PLA layers and the introduction of $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs, leading to the remarkably decreased water vapor permeability of the PHZ-0 films. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and CuO@ZIF-8 NPs provided the composite films with obvious UV-blocking ability. Furthermore, the cherry tomato preservation experiment showed that the composite double-layer films exhibited obvious microbial growth inhibition effect because of the superior bacterial sterilization provided by $\mathrm{CuO}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ NPs and HACC. The cytotoxicity evaluation confirmed the biocompati­ bility of the films. Overall, the well-designed multifunctional doublelayer PT/PHZ films was expected to be a candidate in the field of biodegradable food packaging.
